      "text": "Morph Fast Context subagent performed search on repository:\n\nRelevant context found:\n- fando/README.md:*\n- fando-plan/SKILL.md:*\n- fando-verify/SKILL.md:*\n- fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py:*\n- fando-plan/scripts/codex_lm.py:*\n- fando-verify/scripts/find_plan.py:*\n- fando-verify/scripts/gather_implementation.py:*\n- fando-plan/references/profiles/api.md:*\n- fando-plan/references/profiles/architect.md:*\n- fando-plan/references/profiles/data.md:*\n- fando-plan/references/profiles/devops.md:*\n- fando-plan/references/profiles/security.md:*\n- fando-plan/references/review_prompts.md:*\n- fando-verify/references/verification_prompts.md:*\n- fando-plan/pyproject.toml:*\n\nHere is the content of files:\n\n<file path=\"fando/README.md\">\n1| # Fando\n2| \n3| Claude and Codex - the odd couple of AI-assisted development.\n4| \n5| Like Felix and Oscar from *The Odd Couple*, these two AI systems have different strengths and work better together:\n6| \n7| - **Claude** (the orchestrator) - handles planning, user interaction, code generation, and workflow management\n8| - **Codex** (the reviewer) - provides external review, catches blind spots, and validates implementations\n9| \n10| Fando is a collection of Claude Code skills that leverage this collaboration.\n11| \n12| ## Skills\n13| \n14| ### `/fando-plan`\n15| \n16| Iteratively refines implementation plans using Codex as a reviewer. Uses **autonomous iteration** ([Ralph-style](https://ghuntley.com/ralph/)) - once you consent, Claude loops automatically until the plan is approved.\n17| \n18| **Workflow:**\n19| 1. Claude creates initial plan for your task\n20| 2. Codex reviews and flags issues (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW risk)\n21| 3. Claude addresses feedback and resubmits\n22| 4. **Loop automatically** until LGTM or max iterations (no user input needed between iterations)\n23| \n24| **Features:**\n25| - Autonomous iteration - no babysitting required\n26| - Risk classification (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW)\n27| - Parallel specialist reviewers (Security, Frontend, API, Data, DevOps, Performance)\n28| - Focused prompts - reviewers get full context but stay in their lane\n29| - Canopy integration for token-efficient codebase exploration (large repos)\n30| - Secret detection and redaction before sending to external API\n31| - Plan history saved to `~/.claude/plan-reviews/`\n32| \n33| ### `/fando-verify`\n34| \n35| **Single-pass diagnostic** that verifies your implementation against its plan. Codex compares what you built to what you planned and categorizes each item.\n36| \n37| **Categories:**\n38| \n39| | Category | Description |\n40| |----------|-------------|\n41| | **MATCH** | Implemented exactly as planned |\n42| | **IMPROVEMENT** | Deviation that's better than planned |\n43| | **REGRESSION** | Deviation that's worse than planned |\n44| | **MISSING** | Planned but not implemented |\n45| | **UNPLANNED** | Implemented but not in plan |\n46| \n47| **After verification, you decide:**\n48| - Regressions/missing items? → Fix them, re-verify\n49| - Major scope change needed? → Run `/fando-plan` again\n50| - All good? → Ship it\n51| \n52| **Features:**\n53| - Auto-detects plans from `~/.claude/plan-reviews/`\n54| - Gathers implementation diff via git\n55| - Highlights items needing attention\n56| - Saves verification reports\n57| \n58| ## Installation\n59| \n60| Clone to your Claude Code skills directory:\n61| \n62| ```bash\n63| git clone https://github.com/vu1n/fando.git ~/.claude/skills\n64| ```\n65| \n66| Or add to an existing skills directory:\n67| \n68| ```bash\n69| cd ~/.claude/skills\n70| git remote add origin https://github.com/vu1n/fando.git\n71| git pull origin main\n72| ```\n73| \n74| ## Requirements\n75| \n76| - Python 3.10+\n77| - [OpenAI Codex CLI](https://github.com/openai/codex) installed and configured\n78| - Git (for implementation verification)\n79| \n80| ### Optional (Recommended for Large Codebases)\n81| \n82| - [Canopy v0.1.0](https://github.com/vu1n/canopy/releases/tag/v0.1.0) - Token-efficient codebase indexing for repos >1000 files. Enables 68% token reduction through handle-based exploration vs full file reads.\n83| \n84| ## Usage\n85| \n86| ### Plan a Feature\n87| \n88| ```\n89| /fando-plan Add JWT authentication with refresh tokens\n90| ```\n91| \n92| Claude creates a plan, Codex reviews it, and they iterate until the plan is solid.\n93| \n94| ### Verify Your Implementation\n95| \n96| After implementing, verify it matches the plan:\n97| \n98| ```\n99| /fando-verify\n100| ```\n101| \n102| Or with explicit plan path:\n103| \n104| ```\n105| /fando-verify --path=~/.claude/plan-reviews/my-project/2026-01-19-auth.md\n106| ```\n107| \n108| ## Typical Workflow\n109| \n110| ```\n111| ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n112| │  /fando-plan Add user authentication                        │\n113| │      ↓                                                      │\n114| │  [Autonomous loop - no user input needed]                   │\n115| │      Claude creates plan → Codex reviews → Claude refines   │\n116| │      ↓                                                      │\n117| │  Plan approved & saved to ~/.claude/plan-reviews/           │\n118| └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n119|                            ↓\n120| ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n121| │  Implement the feature (with Claude's help)                 │\n122| └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n123|                            ↓\n124| ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n125| │  /fando-verify                                              │\n126| │      ↓                                                      │\n127| │  [Single pass - diagnostic checkpoint]                      │\n128| │      Codex compares implementation to plan                  │\n129| │      ↓                                                      │\n130| │  Results: matches, improvements, regressions, missing       │\n131| └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n132|                            ↓\n133| ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n134| │  You decide:                                                │\n135| │    • Regressions/missing? → Fix & re-verify                 │\n136| │    • Major change needed? → /fando-plan again               │\n137| │    • All good? → Ship it                                    │\n138| └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n139| ```\n140| \n141| ## Directory Structure\n142| \n143| ```\n144| ~/.claude/skills/\n145| ├── fando-plan/\n146| │   ├── SKILL.md\n147| │   ├── scripts/\n148| │   │   ├── call_codex.py           # Codex CLI wrapper\n149| │   │   ├── parse_findings.py       # Parse review response\n150| │   │   ├── secrets.py              # Secret detection\n151| │   │   └── run_parallel_reviews.py # Parallel reviewer orchestration\n152| │   ├── references/\n153| │   └── examples/\n154| ├── fando-verify/\n155| │   ├── SKILL.md\n156| │   ├── scripts/\n157| │   │   ├── find_plan.py           # Auto-detect plans\n158| │   │   ├── gather_implementation.py  # Git diff collector\n159| │   │   └── parse_verification.py  # Parse verification\n160| │   ├── references/\n161| │   └── examples/\n162| └── README.md\n163| ```\n164| \n165| ## Why \"Fando\"?\n166| \n167| **F**elix **and** **O**scar - the characters from *The Odd Couple*. Claude and Codex are different AI systems with different approaches, but they complement each other well. One plans and builds, the other reviews and validates.\n168| \n169| ## License\n170| \n171| MIT\n172| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/SKILL.md\">\n1| # Skill: /fando-plan\n2| \n3| Automates the workflow of creating a plan, sending it to OpenAI Codex for review, and iterating until the plan is refined. Part of the Fando toolkit - Claude and Codex working together as the \"odd couple\" of AI-assisted development.\n4| \n5| ## Trigger\n6| \n7| User runs `/fando-plan [--security=<level>] <task description>`\n8| \n9| **Options:**\n10| - `--security=personal` - Minimal security review (side projects)\n11| - `--security=internal` - Standard security (internal tools)\n12| - `--security=public` - Strict security (customer-facing) [default]\n13| - `--security=enterprise` - Maximum security (regulated/compliance)\n14| \n15| ## Autonomous Iteration (Ralph-style)\n16| \n17| **IMPORTANT**: This skill uses autonomous iteration. Once the user consents to start, Claude should:\n18| \n19| 1. **Iterate automatically** - Do NOT ask for user input between iterations\n20| 2. **Keep looping** - Continue refining the plan until a stop condition is met\n21| 3. **Show progress** - Display each iteration's feedback and plan updates as you go\n22| 4. **Only pause when necessary** - See stop conditions below\n23| \n24| This follows the \"Ralph method\" philosophy: iterate continuously, accept imperfection, refine until done.\n25| \n26| ## Workflow\n27| \n28| ### Phase 1: Consent & Initialization\n29| \n30| 1. **Check Codex CLI availability:**\n31|    ```bash\n32|    codex --version\n33|    ```\n34|    If not installed, inform user: \"Codex CLI not found. Install from https://github.com/openai/codex\"\n35| \n36| 2. **Explore codebase** to inform the plan (see [Codebase Exploration Strategy](#codebase-exploration-strategy) below)\n37| \n38| 3. **Create initial plan** for the user's task using insights from exploration\n39| \n40| 4. **Scan for secrets** before proceeding:\n41|    ```bash\n42|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/secrets.py --mode=check <<< \"$PLAN\"\n43|    ```\n44|    - If secrets found, show warning and offer options:\n45|      - [Redact and proceed] - mask secrets before sending\n46|      - [Cancel] - abort the review\n47|      - [I understand, send anyway] - proceed with secrets (user acknowledges risk)\n48| \n49| 5. **Detect or confirm security level:**\n50|    ```bash\n51|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/detect_security_level.py <<< \"$PLAN\"\n52|    ```\n53| \n54|    - If `--security=X` flag provided: use that level\n55|    - If auto-detected with high confidence (>0.7): use detected level\n56|    - Otherwise: show detected level and ask user to confirm:\n57|      ```\n58|      Detected security level: internal (matched: \"admin dashboard\", \"employee\")\n59| \n60|      Options:\n61|      1. Yes, use internal\n62|      2. Change to: personal / public / enterprise\n63|      ```\n64| \n65|    Display the level being used:\n66|    ```\n67|    Security level: internal\n68|    └─ Auth issues flagged, compliance checks skipped\n69|    ```\n70| \n71| 6. **Show consent prompt:**\n72|    ```\n73|    This will send your plan to Codex for review.\n74| \n75|    - The plan will be sent to OpenAI's Codex API\n76|    - A copy will be saved to ~/.claude/plan-reviews/\n77| \n78|    Options:\n79|    1. Yes, proceed\n80|    2. No, cancel\n81|    3. Yes, but don't log\n82|    ```\n83|    Only proceed if user consents.\n84| \n85| ### Phase 2: Parallel Specialist Reviews (NEW)\n86| \n87| After initial plan creation, run specialized reviewers in parallel for domain-specific feedback.\n88| \n89| #### 2.1 Detect Relevant Profiles\n90| \n91| ```bash\n92| python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/detect_profiles.py <<< \"$PLAN\"\n93| ```\n94| \n95| Returns profiles based on plan content (e.g., `[\"security\", \"frontend\", \"api\"]`).\n96| \n97| **Available Profiles:**\n98| \n99| | Profile | Trigger Keywords | Focus Areas |\n100| |---------|------------------|-------------|\n101| | **Security** | auth, password, token, jwt, encrypt, secret, permission, role | Auth, authz, input validation, secrets, OWASP |\n102| | **Frontend** | react, vue, component, css, ui, ux, form, modal, state, hook | Components, state, accessibility, UX patterns |\n103| | **Data** | database, schema, migration, query, sql, table, index, postgres | Schema design, queries, indexes, consistency |\n104| | **API** | endpoint, rest, graphql, route, request, response, http, webhook | Contract design, versioning, error handling |\n105| | **DevOps** | deploy, ci/cd, docker, k8s, pipeline, terraform, aws, monitoring | Infrastructure, deployment, observability |\n106| | **Performance** | cache, optimize, latency, throughput, scale, load, memory | Bottlenecks, caching, optimization strategies |\n107| \n108| #### 2.2 Display Selected Reviewers\n109| \n110| ```\n111| Detected plan components: authentication, React UI, REST endpoints\n112| \n113| Running specialist reviewers in parallel:\n114|   ├─ Security Reviewer\n115|   ├─ Frontend Architect\n116|   └─ API Designer\n117| ```\n118| \n119| #### 2.3 Parallel-then-Merge Iteration Loop (max 5 iterations)\n120| \n121| **FOR EACH ITERATION:**\n122| \n123| 1. **Run ALL selected reviewers in parallel:**\n124|    ```bash\n125|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/run_parallel_reviews.py \\\n126|      --security-level=$SECURITY_LEVEL \\\n127|      security frontend api <<< \"$PLAN\"\n128|    ```\n129| \n130| 2. **Aggregate findings from all reviewers:**\n131|    ```bash\n132|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/aggregate_findings.py <<< \"$PARALLEL_RESULTS\"\n133|    ```\n134| \n135| 3. **Display findings grouped by reviewer:**\n136|    ```\n137|    ━━━ Iteration 1/5 Findings ━━━\n138| \n139|    Security Reviewer:\n140|    - [HIGH] Missing CSRF protection on auth endpoints\n141|    - [MEDIUM] JWT expiry too long (24h → recommend 1h)\n142| \n143|    Frontend Architect:\n144|    - [MEDIUM] No loading states defined for async operations\n145| \n146|    API Designer:\n147|    - [MEDIUM] Missing rate limiting on public endpoints\n148| \n149|    ⚠️  Potential Conflict Detected:\n150|    - Security: \"Strict rate limit 10/min\"\n151|    - Performance: \"Rate limit too aggressive\"\n152|    💡 Will resolve in architect review\n153|    ```\n154| \n155| 4. **Claude updates plan ONCE to address ALL findings**\n156| \n157| 5. **Check stop conditions:**\n158| \n159|    | Condition | Action |\n160|    |-----------|--------|\n161|    | 0 HIGH + 0 MEDIUM from all reviewers | **Stop** - proceed to Phase 2.4 (Architect Review) |\n162|    | HIGH or MEDIUM findings remain | **Continue** - loop back to step 1 |\n163|    | Same findings repeated 2x | **Stop** - surface to user |\n164|    | 5 iterations reached | **Stop** - proceed to architect with remaining issues |\n165| \n166| 6. **Track what was addressed:**\n167|    ```\n168|    ━━━ Iteration 1 → 2 Changes ━━━\n169|    ✓ [ADDRESSED] CSRF protection → Added to auth middleware spec\n170|    ✓ [ADDRESSED] JWT expiry → Changed to 1h with refresh tokens\n171|    ✓ [ADDRESSED] Loading states → Added UX section with states\n172|    ✓ [ADDRESSED] Rate limiting → Added to API spec\n173|    ```\n174| \n175| 7. **Collect training data** (if logging enabled):\n176|    For each reviewer profile, save a training example:\n177|    ```bash\n178|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/collect_training.py \\\n179|      --domain $PROFILE --other-domains \"$OTHER_PROFILES\" \\\n180|      --security-level $SECURITY_LEVEL \\\n181|      --plan-id \"$PROJECT-$DATE\" --iteration $ITER \\\n182|      --plan-before $PLAN_BEFORE_FILE \\\n183|      --plan-after $PLAN_AFTER_FILE \\\n184|      <<< \"$REVIEW_OUTPUT_FOR_PROFILE\"\n185|    ```\n186| \n187| #### 2.4 Final Systems Architect Review\n188| \n189| After all specialists are satisfied (0 HIGH/MEDIUM findings):\n190| \n191| 1. **Run architect final pass:**\n192|    ```bash\n193|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py \\\n194|      \"$(cat ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/references/profiles/architect.md)\" <<< \"$PLAN\"\n195|    ```\n196| \n197| 2. **Architect reviews:**\n198|    - Complete aggregated plan\n199|    - Conflicts between specialist recommendations\n200|    - Cross-cutting concerns\n201|    - Overall coherence\n202| \n203| 3. **If conflicts detected, resolve them:**\n204|    ```\n205|    ⚠️  Conflict Detected:\n206|    - Security: \"Strict rate limit 10/min\"\n207|    - Performance: \"Rate limit too aggressive for expected load\"\n208| \n209|    Architect Resolution: \"Use 30/min with burst allowance of 50\"\n210|    ```\n211| \n212| 4. **Final LGTM or one more iteration**\n213| \n214| ### Phase 2 (Legacy): Single Reviewer Loop\n215| \n216| **Note:** The legacy single-reviewer loop is still available for simpler plans or when specialist profiles aren't detected.\n217| \n218| **Loop automatically without user input.** For each iteration:\n219| \n220| 1. **Build the review prompt** with:\n221|    - Current plan (inline)\n222|    - Previous feedback summary (last 2-3 iterations)\n223|    - Risk-level classification instructions\n224| \n225| 2. **Call Codex for review:**\n226|    ```bash\n227|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py \"$REVIEW_PROMPT\" <<< \"$PLAN\"\n228|    ```\n229| \n230| 3. **Parse the response:**\n231|    ```bash\n232|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/parse_findings.py <<< \"$CODEX_RESPONSE\"\n233|    ```\n234| \n235| 4. **Display iteration results** to user (but do NOT wait for input):\n236|    ```\n237|    Iteration 2/5 - Codex feedback:\n238|    - [HIGH] Missing error handling for network failures\n239|    - [MEDIUM] Consider rate limiting\n240| \n241|    Addressing feedback...\n242|    ```\n243| \n244| 5. **Check stop conditions:**\n245| \n246|    | Condition | Action |\n247|    |-----------|--------|\n248|    | LGTM or 0 HIGH + 0 MEDIUM | **Stop** - plan approved, proceed to Phase 3 |\n249|    | HIGH or MEDIUM findings | **Continue automatically** - update plan and loop |\n250|    | Only LOW/NITPICK remaining | **Stop** - plan approved (minor issues noted) |\n251|    | Same HIGH/MEDIUM repeated 2x | **Stop** - Codex is stuck, surface to user |\n252|    | 5 iterations reached | **Stop** - ask user if they want to continue |\n253| \n254| 6. **If continuing:** Update the plan to address feedback, then **immediately loop back to step 1**\n255| \n256| ### Phase 3: Documentation\n257| \n258| If user consented to logging:\n259| \n260| 1. Get project name:\n261|    ```bash\n262|    git remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null | sed 's/.*\\///' | sed 's/\\.git$//' || basename \"$(pwd)\"\n263|    ```\n264| \n265| 2. Save documentation to:\n266|    ```\n267|    ~/.claude/plan-reviews/{project-name}/{YYYY-MM-DD}-{task-slug}.md\n268|    ```\n269| \n270| 3. Include:\n271|    - Original task\n272|    - Each iteration's plan version\n273|    - Each iteration's Codex feedback\n274|    - How feedback was addressed\n275|    - Final approved plan\n276|    - Key learnings\n277| \n278| ## Review Prompt Template\n279| \n280| Use the prompts from `references/review_prompts.md`:\n281| - **Initial review**: Full architectural review\n282| - **Iteration review**: Focus on whether previous concerns were addressed\n283| \n284| ## Output Format\n285| \n286| Show progress continuously (no pausing for input during iteration):\n287| \n288| ```\n289| Creating initial plan for \"{task}\"...\n290| [Shows plan]\n291| \n292| Detecting relevant reviewers...\n293| Found: Security, Frontend, API\n294| \n295| ━━━ Parallel Review Loop ━━━\n296| \n297| ━━━ Iteration 1/5 ━━━\n298| Running reviewers in parallel...\n299|   Security Reviewer: reviewing...\n300|   Frontend Architect: reviewing...\n301|   API Designer: reviewing...\n302| \n303| Findings:\n304|   Security:\n305|   - [HIGH] Missing CSRF protection on auth endpoints\n306|   - [MEDIUM] JWT expiry too long (24h)\n307| \n308|   Frontend:\n309|   - [MEDIUM] No loading states defined\n310| \n311|   API:\n312|   - [MEDIUM] Missing rate limiting\n313| \n314| Addressing 4 findings...\n315| [Shows key plan changes]\n316| \n317| ━━━ Iteration 2/5 ━━━\n318| Running reviewers in parallel...\n319| \n320| Findings:\n321|   Security:\n322|   - [LOW] Consider refresh token rotation\n323| \n324|   Frontend:\n325|   - [LOW] Consider optimistic updates\n326| \n327|   API:\n328|   ✓ No issues\n329| \n330| All HIGH/MEDIUM addressed. Proceeding to final review.\n331| \n332| ━━━ Systems Architect (Final Pass) ━━━\n333| Reviewing complete plan...\n334| Checking for conflicts...\n335| No conflicts detected.\n336| \n337| Remaining LOW findings (optional):\n338| - [LOW] Security: refresh token rotation\n339| - [LOW] Frontend: optimistic updates\n340| \n341| ✓ LGTM - Plan approved\n342| \n343| ━━━ Final Result ━━━\n344| Plan approved after 2 specialist iterations + 1 architect pass.\n345| Documentation saved to: ~/.claude/plan-reviews/my-project/2026-01-21-jwt-dashboard.md\n346| \n347| [Shows final plan]\n348| ```\n349| \n350| ## Error Handling\n351| \n352| | Error | Action |\n353| |-------|--------|\n354| | Codex CLI not found | Show installation instructions |\n355| | Codex timeout (>10min) | Retry once, then ask user |\n356| | Codex returns error | Show error, ask user how to proceed |\n357| | Parsing fails | Show raw response, continue with manual review |\n358| | Secrets detected | Block by default, offer redact option |\n359| \n360| ## Configuration\n361| \n362| - **Max iterations**: 5 (configurable)\n363| - **Timeout**: 10 minutes per Codex call\n364| - **Min Codex version**: v0.85.0\n365| \n366| ## Reviewer Focus Strategy\n367| \n368| Each specialist reviewer receives the **full plan** for context understanding, but is instructed to only flag issues in their specific domain. This approach:\n369| \n370| - **Preserves reasoning**: Reviewers understand WHY decisions were made\n371| - **Prevents duplicates**: Each specialist stays in their lane\n372| - **Enables cross-domain awareness**: Reviewers can note dependencies without flagging other domains' choices\n373| \n374| A focus preamble is automatically added to each reviewer prompt explaining their role in the multi-reviewer setup.\n375| \n376| ## Codebase Exploration Strategy\n377| \n378| Before creating a plan, Claude should explore the codebase to understand existing architecture, patterns, and relevant code. Use the appropriate tool based on codebase size and query type.\n379| \n380| ### Decision Framework\n381| \n382| ```\n383| ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n384| │  Is the codebase large (>1000 files)?                       │\n385| │     ↓ Yes                              ↓ No                 │\n386| │  ┌─────────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────────────┐ │\n387| │  │ Use Canopy          │    │ Use native tools            │ │\n388| │  │ (token-efficient)   │    │ (Grep, Glob, Read)          │ │\n389| │  └─────────────────────┘    └─────────────────────────────┘ │\n390| └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n391| ```\n392| \n393| ### Tool Selection Guide\n394| \n395| | Query Type | Large Repo (>1000 files) | Small Repo (<500 files) |\n396| |------------|--------------------------|-------------------------|\n397| | \"How does X work?\" | `canopy_query` with pattern | Grep + Read |\n398| | Find symbol definition | `canopy_query` with symbol | Grep for function/class |\n399| | Cross-file tracing | `canopy_query` → `canopy_expand` | Grep + manual Read |\n400| | Known file path | Read directly | Read directly |\n401| | Literal text search | Grep | Grep |\n402| | File name pattern | Glob | Glob |\n403| \n404| ### Using Canopy (Large Codebases)\n405| \n406| **Step 1: Check if indexed**\n407| ```\n408| mcp__canopy__canopy_status(path=\"/path/to/repo\")\n409| ```\n410| \n411| **Step 2: Query for relevant code**\n412| ```\n413| # Symbol search (functions, classes)\n414| mcp__canopy__canopy_query(path=\"/path/to/repo\", symbol=\"AuthController\")\n415| \n416| # Pattern search (concepts, keywords)\n417| mcp__canopy__canopy_query(path=\"/path/to/repo\", pattern=\"authentication\")\n418| \n419| # Scoped search\n420| mcp__canopy__canopy_query(path=\"/path/to/repo\", pattern=\"login\", glob=\"src/**/*.ts\")\n421| ```\n422| \n423| **Step 3: Expand relevant handles**\n424| ```\n425| mcp__canopy__canopy_expand(path=\"/path/to/repo\", handle_ids=[\"h1a2b3c4d5e6\"])\n426| ```\n427| \n428| ### Benefits of Canopy for Planning\n429| \n430| - **68% token reduction** through handle-based previews vs full file reads\n431| - **2.3x more detailed** symbol discovery\n432| - **Cross-file tracing** without reading entire files\n433| - **Predictive indexing** - no blocking on first query\n434| \n435| ### When to Skip Canopy\n436| \n437| - Codebase under 500 files (native tools are fast enough)\n438| - Looking for literal text patterns (Grep is faster)\n439| - Reading known file paths (Read directly)\n440| - Simple file discovery (Glob is sufficient)\n441| \n442| ## DSPy Review Backend\n443| \n444| The default review backend uses DSPy Signatures with per-domain docstrings encoding\n445| reviewer expertise. This replaces the legacy markdown prompt approach with structured,\n446| optimizable prompts.\n447| \n448| ### Backend Selection\n449| \n450| ```bash\n451| # DSPy backend (default) — structured Signatures via CodexLM adapter\n452| echo \"$PLAN\" | python3 run_parallel_reviews.py --backend=dspy security api\n453| \n454| # Legacy codex backend — hand-crafted markdown prompts\n455| echo \"$PLAN\" | python3 run_parallel_reviews.py --backend=codex security api\n456| \n457| # DSPy without GEPA-optimized modules\n458| echo \"$PLAN\" | python3 run_parallel_reviews.py --backend=dspy --no-optimized security api\n459| ```\n460| \n461| If DSPy is not installed, the CLI falls back to the codex backend automatically.\n462| \n463| ### Architecture\n464| \n465| ```\n466| detect_profiles → DSPy DomainReviewModule.forward()\n467|                     → CodexLM adapter\n468|                       → codex exec (subprocess)\n469|                     → dspy.Prediction\n470|                   → prediction_to_review_output()\n471|                     → parse_findings() (unchanged)\n472| ```\n473| \n474| Each domain has its own DSPy Signature class with a rich docstring derived from\n475| `references/profiles/*.md`. The docstrings are the source of truth for reviewer\n476| behavior; the markdown files are kept as human-readable documentation.\n477| \n478| ### GEPA Prompt Optimization\n479| \n480| Training data is collected automatically during plan review sessions. Once you\n481| have enough labeled examples (10+ per domain, 50+ recommended), you can optimize\n482| reviewer prompts using GEPA.\n483| \n484| ```bash\n485| # Check training data status\n486| python3 optimize.py --stats\n487| \n488| # Optimize a single domain\n489| python3 optimize.py --domain security --reflection-model openai/gpt-4o --auto light\n490| \n491| # Optimize all domains with sufficient data\n492| python3 optimize.py --all --reflection-model openai/gpt-4o\n493| \n494| # Export optimized instructions to markdown for inspection\n495| python3 optimize.py --export\n496| ```\n497| \n498| **Note:** GEPA requires an API key for the reflection model (one-time optimization\n499| cost). Runtime inference uses Codex CLI — no API keys needed.\n500| \n501| ### Training Data & Optimized Modules\n502| \n503| | Path | Purpose |\n504| |------|---------|\n505| | `~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/training_data/*.json` | Labeled training examples |\n506| | `~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/optimized/{domain}.json` | GEPA-optimized module state |\n507| \n508| ## Files\n509| \n510| | File | Purpose |\n511| |------|---------|\n512| | `scripts/call_codex.py` | Safe Codex invocation via stdin |\n513| | `scripts/parse_findings.py` | Extract risk counts from response |\n514| | `scripts/secrets.py` | Secret detection and redaction |\n515| | `scripts/detect_profiles.py` | Analyze plan, return relevant reviewer profiles |\n516| | `scripts/detect_security_level.py` | Detect security level from plan content |\n517| | `scripts/run_parallel_reviews.py` | Orchestrate parallel Codex calls with focus prompts |\n518| | `scripts/aggregate_findings.py` | Merge and dedupe findings from all reviewers |\n519| | `scripts/dspy_reviewers.py` | DSPy Signatures, Modules, metrics, training data collection |\n520| | `scripts/codex_lm.py` | CodexLM adapter — DSPy LM provider wrapping codex exec |\n521| | `scripts/optimize.py` | GEPA optimization CLI for reviewer prompts |\n522| | `scripts/backfill_training.py` | Backfill training data from existing plan review logs |\n523| | `scripts/collect_training.py` | Collect training data during plan review iterations |\n524| | `pyproject.toml` | Project dependencies (dspy optional) |\n525| | `references/review_prompts.md` | Generic prompt templates (legacy reference) |\n526| | `references/profiles/security.md` | Security reviewer prompt |\n527| | `references/profiles/frontend.md` | Frontend architect prompt |\n528| | `references/profiles/data.md` | Data architect prompt |\n529| | `references/profiles/api.md` | API designer prompt |\n530| | `references/profiles/devops.md` | DevOps engineer prompt |\n531| | `references/profiles/performance.md` | Performance engineer prompt |\n532| | `references/profiles/architect.md` | Final systems architect prompt |\n533| | `examples/sample_session.md` | Example workflow |\n534| \n535| ### Project-level Files\n536| \n537| | File | Purpose |\n538| |------|---------|\n539| | `.claude/settings.local.json` | Claude Code project settings (if needed) |\n540| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-verify/SKILL.md\">\n1| # Skill: /fando-verify\n2| \n3| Verifies an implementation against its plan using OpenAI Codex, identifying matches, improvements, regressions, and missing items. Closes the feedback loop with `/fando-plan`. Part of the Fando toolkit - Claude and Codex working together as the \"odd couple\" of AI-assisted development.\n4| \n5| ## Trigger\n6| \n7| User runs `/fando-verify [plan-path]`\n8| \n9| If no plan path is provided, the skill auto-detects the plan from `~/.claude/plan-reviews/{project}/`.\n10| \n11| ## Workflow\n12| \n13| ### Phase 1: Plan Discovery\n14| \n15| 1. **Check Codex CLI availability:**\n16|    ```bash\n17|    codex --version\n18|    ```\n19|    If not installed, inform user: \"Codex CLI not found. Install from https://github.com/openai/codex\"\n20| \n21| 2. **Find the plan to verify against:**\n22|    ```bash\n23|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-verify/scripts/find_plan.py [explicit-path]\n24|    ```\n25|    - If explicit path provided, use that\n26|    - Otherwise, find latest plan in `~/.claude/plan-reviews/{project}/`\n27|    - If no plan found, ask user to provide one\n28| \n29| 3. **Show discovered plan** to user for confirmation\n30| \n31| ### Phase 2: Implementation Gathering\n32| \n33| 1. **Gather implementation diff:**\n34|    ```bash\n35|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-verify/scripts/gather_implementation.py [--ref=<commit>]\n36|    ```\n37|    - Default: diff against merge-base with main/master\n38|    - Optional: diff against specific commit/ref\n39| \n40| 2. **Display summary** to user:\n41|    ```\n42|    Found: 8 files changed, 245 additions, 32 deletions\n43|    Base: abc1234 (merge-base with main)\n44|    ```\n45| \n46| ### Phase 3: Consent & Verification\n47| \n48| 1. **Scan for secrets** in both plan and diff:\n49|    ```bash\n50|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/secrets.py --mode=check <<< \"$CONTENT\"\n51|    ```\n52|    - If secrets found, show warning and offer options:\n53|      - [Redact and proceed] - mask secrets before sending\n54|      - [Cancel] - abort verification\n55|      - [I understand, send anyway] - proceed with secrets\n56| \n57| 2. **Show consent prompt:**\n58|    ```\n59|    This will send your plan and code diff to Codex for verification.\n60| \n61|    - Plan: ~/.claude/plan-reviews/my-api/2026-01-19-jwt-auth.md\n62|    - Diff: 8 files, 245 additions, 32 deletions\n63| \n64|    Options:\n65|    1. Yes, proceed\n66|    2. No, cancel\n67|    ```\n68|    Only proceed if user consents.\n69| \n70| 3. **Build verification prompt** using `references/verification_prompts.md`:\n71|    - Include original plan\n72|    - Include implementation diff\n73|    - Include categorization instructions\n74| \n75| 4. **Call Codex:**\n76|    ```bash\n77|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py \"$VERIFICATION_PROMPT\" <<< \"$PLAN_AND_DIFF\"\n78|    ```\n79| \n80| 5. **Parse results:**\n81|    ```bash\n82|    python3 ~/.claude/skills/fando-verify/scripts/parse_verification.py <<< \"$CODEX_RESPONSE\"\n83|    ```\n84| \n85| ### Phase 4: Results & Documentation\n86| \n87| 1. **Display categorized results** to user:\n88|    - MATCH items\n89|    - IMPROVEMENT items (with explanation)\n90|    - REGRESSION items (highlighted as needing attention)\n91|    - MISSING items (planned but not implemented)\n92|    - UNPLANNED items (implemented but not in plan)\n93| \n94| 2. **Show summary:**\n95|    ```\n96|    2 matches, 1 improvement, 1 regression, 1 missing, 1 unplanned\n97|    ```\n98| \n99| 3. **Highlight attention items:**\n100|    ```\n101|    Attention needed:\n102|    - Rate limiting is looser than planned (10/min vs 5/min)\n103|    - Token blacklist not implemented (required for secure logout)\n104|    ```\n105| \n106| 4. **Save verification report:**\n107|    ```\n108|    ~/.claude/plan-reviews/{project}/{YYYY-MM-DD}-{task-slug}-verify.md\n109|    ```\n110| \n111| ## Verification Categories\n112| \n113| | Category | Marker | Description |\n114| |----------|--------|-------------|\n115| | **MATCH** | `[MATCH]` | Implementation matches plan exactly |\n116| | **IMPROVEMENT** | `[IMPROVEMENT]` | Deviation that's better than planned (more robust, secure, clean) |\n117| | **REGRESSION** | `[REGRESSION]` | Deviation that's worse than planned (missing functionality, less secure) |\n118| | **MISSING** | `[MISSING]` | Planned item not implemented at all |\n119| | **UNPLANNED** | `[UNPLANNED]` | Implemented but not mentioned in plan |\n120| \n121| ## Output Format\n122| \n123| Show progress to user throughout:\n124| ```\n125| Looking for plan to verify against...\n126| Found: ~/.claude/plan-reviews/my-api/2026-01-19-jwt-auth.md\n127| \n128| Gathering implementation diff (comparing to main)...\n129| Found 8 files changed, 245 additions, 32 deletions.\n130| \n131| This will send your plan and code diff to Codex for verification.\n132| [Yes, proceed] [No, cancel]\n133| \n134| Sending to Codex for verification...\n135| \n136| ## Verification Results\n137| \n138| - [MATCH] User model with password hashing - bcrypt implementation correct\n139| - [MATCH] Login endpoint - Returns JWT as planned\n140| - [IMPROVEMENT] Refresh tokens - Added token family ID for logout-all-devices\n141| - [REGRESSION] Rate limiting - Only 10/min instead of planned 5/min\n142| - [MISSING] Token blacklist - Redis integration not implemented\n143| - [UNPLANNED] /whoami endpoint - Returns current user info\n144| \n145| ## Summary\n146| 2 matches, 1 improvement, 1 regression, 1 missing, 1 unplanned\n147| \n148| Attention needed:\n149| - Rate limiting is looser than planned (10/min vs 5/min)\n150| - Token blacklist not implemented (required for secure logout)\n151| \n152| Verification saved to: ~/.claude/plan-reviews/my-api/2026-01-19-jwt-auth-verify.md\n153| ```\n154| \n155| ## Error Handling\n156| \n157| | Error | Action |\n158| |-------|--------|\n159| | Codex CLI not found | Show installation instructions |\n160| | No plan found | Ask user to provide plan path or content |\n161| | Not in git repo | Ask user to specify diff manually or provide file list |\n162| | No changes found | Inform user, offer to compare working tree |\n163| | Codex timeout (>10min) | Retry once, then ask user |\n164| | Codex returns error | Show error, ask user how to proceed |\n165| | Parsing fails | Show raw response, continue with manual review |\n166| | Secrets detected | Block by default, offer redact option |\n167| \n168| ## Configuration\n169| \n170| - **Timeout**: 10 minutes per Codex call\n171| - **Plan search path**: `~/.claude/plan-reviews/{project}/`\n172| \n173| ## Related Code Discovery (Optional)\n174| \n175| For large codebases (>1000 files), use Canopy to discover related code that may have been affected by the implementation. This enhances verification by finding:\n176| \n177| - **Callers of modified functions** - Did they handle new return types/errors?\n178| - **Implementations of changed interfaces** - Are they still compatible?\n179| - **Related test files** - Were appropriate tests added/updated?\n180| \n181| ### When to Use\n182| \n183| | Scenario | Action |\n184| |----------|--------|\n185| | Large codebase, complex change | Use Canopy to trace dependencies |\n186| | Small codebase or simple change | Skip - git diff is sufficient |\n187| | Plan mentions specific integrations | Query those symbols |\n188| \n189| ### Usage\n190| \n191| ```\n192| # Find references to modified symbols\n193| mcp__canopy__canopy_query(\n194|   path=\"/path/to/repo\",\n195|   symbol=\"AuthService\",\n196|   kind=\"reference\"\n197| )\n198| \n199| # Trace related code\n200| mcp__canopy__canopy_query(\n201|   path=\"/path/to/repo\",\n202|   pattern=\"authentication\",\n203|   glob=\"**/*.test.ts\"\n204| )\n205| ```\n206| \n207| Include discovered related files in the verification context when relevant.\n208| \n209| ## Files\n210| \n211| | File | Purpose |\n212| |------|---------|\n213| | `scripts/find_plan.py` | Auto-detect plan file from project history |\n214| | `scripts/gather_implementation.py` | Collect git diff for verification |\n215| | `scripts/parse_verification.py` | Parse Codex verification response |\n216| | `references/verification_prompts.md` | Prompt templates for verification |\n217| | `examples/sample_verification.md` | Example verification workflow |\n218| \n219| ## Reused Scripts (from fando-plan)\n220| \n221| | Script | Path |\n222| |--------|------|\n223| | `call_codex.py` | `~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py` |\n224| | `secrets.py` | `~/.claude/skills/fando-plan/scripts/secrets.py` |\n225| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/scripts/call_codex.py\">\n1| #!/usr/bin/env python3\n2| \"\"\"\n3| call_codex.py - Calls Codex with plan for review via stdin\n4| \n5| Usage:\n6|     echo \"$PLAN\" | python3 call_codex.py \"Review prompt here\"\n7|     python3 call_codex.py \"Review prompt\" < plan.md\n8| \"\"\"\n9| import subprocess\n10| import sys\n11| from dataclasses import dataclass\n12| from typing import TypedDict\n13| \n14| \n15| @dataclass\n16| class CodexResult:\n17|     stdout: str\n18|     stderr: str\n19|     exit_code: int\n20|     error: str | None = None\n21| \n22| \n23| class CodexCliInfo(TypedDict):\n24|     \"\"\"Return type for verify_codex_cli().\"\"\"\n25|     installed: bool\n26|     version: str | None\n27|     supports_skip_git: bool\n28|     error: str | None\n29| \n30| \n31| def verify_codex_cli() -> CodexCliInfo:\n32|     \"\"\"Check Codex CLI availability and capabilities.\"\"\"\n33|     result = {\n34|         'installed': False,\n35|         'version': None,\n36|         'supports_skip_git': False,\n37|         'error': None\n38|     }\n39| \n40|     try:\n41|         # Check if codex exists\n42|         version_result = subprocess.run(\n43|             ['codex', '--version'],\n44|             capture_output=True,\n45|             text=True,\n46|             timeout=5\n47|         )\n48|         if version_result.returncode == 0:\n49|             result['installed'] = True\n50|             result['version'] = version_result.stdout.strip()\n51| \n52|         # Test --skip-git-repo-check support\n53|         help_result = subprocess.run(\n54|             ['codex', 'exec', '--help'],\n55|             capture_output=True,\n56|             text=True,\n57|             timeout=5\n58|         )\n59|         result['supports_skip_git'] = '--skip-git-repo-check' in help_result.stdout\n60| \n61|     except FileNotFoundError:\n62|         result['error'] = \"Codex CLI not found. Install from https://github.com/openai/codex\"\n63|     except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n64|         result['error'] = \"Codex CLI timed out during capability check\"\n65| \n66|     return result\n67| \n68| \n69| def call_codex(\n70|     prompt: str,\n71|     plan: str,\n72|     timeout: int = 600,\n73| ) -> CodexResult:\n74|     \"\"\"\n75|     Call Codex exec with plan passed via stdin.\n76| \n77|     Args:\n78|         prompt: The review instructions\n79|         plan: The plan content to review\n80|         timeout: Maximum seconds to wait for response\n81| \n82|     Returns:\n83|         CodexResult with stdout, stderr, exit_code, and any error message.\n84|     \"\"\"\n85|     # Build the full prompt\n86|     full_prompt = f\"{prompt}\\n\\n## Plan to Review\\n\\n{plan}\"\n87| \n88|     # Check CLI capabilities first\n89|     cli_info = verify_codex_cli()\n90|     if cli_info['error']:\n91|         return CodexResult(\n92|             stdout=\"\",\n93|             stderr=\"\",\n94|             exit_code=-1,\n95|             error=cli_info['error']\n96|         )\n97| \n98|     # Build command with optional flags\n99|     cmd = ['codex', 'exec']\n100|     if cli_info['supports_skip_git']:\n101|         cmd.append('--skip-git-repo-check')\n102| \n103|     try:\n104|         # Pass prompt content via stdin - Codex reads from stdin when no prompt arg given\n105|         result = subprocess.run(\n106|             cmd,\n107|             input=full_prompt,\n108|             capture_output=True,\n109|             text=True,\n110|             timeout=timeout\n111|         )\n112| \n113|         # Check for errors\n114|         error = None\n115|         if result.returncode != 0:\n116|             error = f\"Codex exited with code {result.returncode}: {result.stderr}\"\n117| \n118|         return CodexResult(\n119|             stdout=result.stdout,\n120|             stderr=result.stderr,\n121|             exit_code=result.returncode,\n122|             error=error\n123|         )\n124| \n125|     except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n126|         return CodexResult(\n127|             stdout=\"\",\n128|             stderr=\"\",\n129|             exit_code=-1,\n130|             error=f\"ERROR: Codex timed out after {timeout}s\"\n131|         )\n132|     except FileNotFoundError:\n133|         return CodexResult(\n134|             stdout=\"\",\n135|             stderr=\"\",\n136|             exit_code=-1,\n137|             error=\"ERROR: 'codex' command not found. Is Codex CLI installed?\"\n138|         )\n139| \n140| \n141| def main() -> None:\n142|     \"\"\"CLI entry point.\"\"\"\n143|     import argparse\n144| \n145|     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n146|         description='Call Codex to review a plan',\n147|         epilog='Plan content is read from stdin'\n148|     )\n149|     parser.add_argument(\n150|         'prompt',\n151|         nargs='?',\n152|         default='Review this plan for architecture, risks, and completeness.',\n153|         help='Review prompt/instructions'\n154|     )\n155|     parser.add_argument(\n156|         '--timeout',\n157|         type=int,\n158|         default=600,\n159|         help='Timeout in seconds (default: 600 / 10 minutes)'\n160|     )\n161|     parser.add_argument(\n162|         '--check-only',\n163|         action='store_true',\n164|         help='Only check Codex CLI availability, do not call'\n165|     )\n166| \n167|     args = parser.parse_args()\n168| \n169|     # Check-only mode for verification\n170|     if args.check_only:\n171|         info = verify_codex_cli()\n172|         if info['error']:\n173|             print(f\"Error: {info['error']}\", file=sys.stderr)\n174|             sys.exit(1)\n175|         print(f\"Codex CLI: {info['version']}\")\n176|         print(f\"Supports --skip-git-repo-check: {info['supports_skip_git']}\")\n177|         sys.exit(0)\n178| \n179|     # Read plan from stdin\n180|     plan = sys.stdin.read()\n181|     if not plan.strip():\n182|         print(\"Error: No plan content provided on stdin\", file=sys.stderr)\n183|         sys.exit(1)\n184| \n185|     # Call Codex\n186|     result = call_codex(args.prompt, plan, timeout=args.timeout)\n187| \n188|     if result.error:\n189|         print(f\"Error: {result.error}\", file=sys.stderr)\n190|         sys.exit(1)\n191| \n192|     # Output the response\n193|     print(result.stdout)\n194| \n195|     # Also output stderr if present (may contain useful info)\n196|     if result.stderr:\n197|         print(f\"\\n[stderr]: {result.stderr}\", file=sys.stderr)\n198| \n199| \n200| if __name__ == '__main__':\n201|     main()\n202| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/scripts/codex_lm.py\">\n1| #!/usr/bin/env python3\n2| \"\"\"\n3| codex_lm.py - DSPy LM adapter for OpenAI Codex CLI\n4| \n5| Wraps `codex exec` as a DSPy language model provider so DSPy Signatures\n6| can use Codex subscriptions without API keys at runtime.\n7| \n8| Usage:\n9|     import dspy\n10|     from codex_lm import CodexLM\n11| \n12|     lm = CodexLM()\n13|     dspy.configure(lm=lm)\n14| \"\"\"\n15| import subprocess\n16| import time\n17| from typing import Any\n18| \n19| from call_codex import verify_codex_cli\n20| \n21| try:\n22|     from dspy.clients.base_lm import BaseLM\n23| except ImportError:\n24|     raise ImportError(\"DSPy is required: uv pip install dspy>=2.6.0\")\n25| \n26| \n27| class CodexLM(BaseLM):\n28|     \"\"\"DSPy LM adapter that routes inference through `codex exec` subprocess.\"\"\"\n29| \n30|     def __init__(self, timeout: int = 600, **kwargs):\n31|         super().__init__(model=\"codex/exec\", model_type=\"chat\", **kwargs)\n32|         self.timeout = timeout\n33| \n34|         # Verify CLI availability at init time\n35|         cli_info = verify_codex_cli()\n36|         if cli_info[\"error\"]:\n37|             raise RuntimeError(f\"Codex CLI not available: {cli_info['error']}\")\n38|         self.cli_version = cli_info[\"version\"]\n39|         self.supports_skip_git = cli_info[\"supports_skip_git\"]\n40| \n41|     def _messages_to_prompt(self, messages: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> str:\n42|         \"\"\"Flatten DSPy chat messages into a single prompt string for codex exec.\n43| \n44|         DSPy sends messages as [{\"role\": \"system\", ...}, {\"role\": \"user\", ...}].\n45|         Codex exec reads a single prompt from stdin, so we concatenate them.\n46|         \"\"\"\n47|         parts = []\n48|         for msg in messages:\n49|             role = msg.get(\"role\", \"user\")\n50|             content = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")\n51|             if role == \"system\":\n52|                 parts.append(content)\n53|             elif role == \"user\":\n54|                 parts.append(content)\n55|             elif role == \"assistant\":\n56|                 # Include prior assistant turns for context\n57|                 parts.append(f\"[Previous response]\\n{content}\")\n58|         return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n59| \n60|     def forward(\n61|         self,\n62|         prompt: str | None = None,\n63|         messages: list[dict[str, Any]] | None = None,\n64|         **kwargs,\n65|     ) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:\n66|         \"\"\"Execute a single LM call through codex exec.\n67| \n68|         Returns OpenAI-compatible response format that DSPy expects.\n69|         \"\"\"\n70|         # Build the text prompt from either raw prompt or messages\n71|         if messages:\n72|             text_prompt = self._messages_to_prompt(messages)\n73|         elif prompt:\n74|             text_prompt = prompt\n75|         else:\n76|             raise ValueError(\"Either prompt or messages must be provided\")\n77| \n78|         # Build subprocess command\n79|         cmd = [\"codex\", \"exec\"]\n80|         if self.supports_skip_git:\n81|             cmd.append(\"--skip-git-repo-check\")\n82| \n83|         # Call codex exec via subprocess\n84|         start = time.time()\n85|         try:\n86|             result = subprocess.run(\n87|                 cmd,\n88|                 input=text_prompt,\n89|                 capture_output=True,\n90|                 text=True,\n91|                 timeout=self.timeout,\n92|             )\n93|         except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n94|             raise RuntimeError(f\"Codex exec timed out after {self.timeout}s\")\n95|         except FileNotFoundError:\n96|             raise RuntimeError(\"codex command not found\")\n97| \n98|         duration = time.time() - start\n99| \n100|         if result.returncode != 0:\n101|             raise RuntimeError(\n102|                 f\"Codex exec failed (code {result.returncode}): {result.stderr}\"\n103|             )\n104| \n105|         output_text = result.stdout.strip()\n106| \n107|         # Return OpenAI chat-completion-compatible format\n108|         response = {\n109|             \"id\": f\"codex-{int(time.time())}\",\n110|             \"object\": \"chat.completion\",\n111|             \"created\": int(time.time()),\n112|             \"model\": \"codex/exec\",\n113|             \"choices\": [\n114|                 {\n115|                     \"index\": 0,\n116|                     \"message\": {\n117|                         \"role\": \"assistant\",\n118|                         \"content\": output_text,\n119|                     },\n120|                     \"finish_reason\": \"stop\",\n121|                 }\n122|             ],\n123|             \"usage\": {\n124|                 \"prompt_tokens\": len(text_prompt.split()),\n125|                 \"completion_tokens\": len(output_text.split()),\n126|                 \"total_tokens\": len(text_prompt.split()) + len(output_text.split()),\n127|             },\n128|         }\n129| \n130|         # Log the call for DSPy history\n131|         self.history.append(\n132|             {\n133|                 \"prompt\": prompt,\n134|                 \"messages\": messages,\n135|                 \"kwargs\": kwargs,\n136|                 \"response\": response,\n137|                 \"outputs\": [output_text],\n138|                 \"usage\": response[\"usage\"],\n139|                 \"cost\": 0.0,\n140|                 \"timestamp\": time.time(),\n141|                 \"duration\": duration,\n142|             }\n143|         )\n144| \n145|         return [output_text]\n146| \n147|     async def aforward(\n148|         self,\n149|         prompt: str | None = None,\n150|         messages: list[dict[str, Any]] | None = None,\n151|         **kwargs,\n152|     ) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:\n153|         \"\"\"Async variant - delegates to sync forward since codex exec is subprocess-based.\"\"\"\n154|         return self.forward(prompt=prompt, messages=messages, **kwargs)\n155| \n156| \n157| if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n158|     lm = CodexLM()\n159|     print(f\"CodexLM initialized: {lm.cli_version}\")\n160|     print(f\"Supports --skip-git-repo-check: {lm.supports_skip_git}\")\n161| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-verify/scripts/find_plan.py\">\n1| #!/usr/bin/env python3\n2| \"\"\"\n3| find_plan.py - Auto-detect the plan to verify against\n4| \n5| Usage:\n6|     python3 find_plan.py                    # Auto-detect for current project\n7|     python3 find_plan.py --path=/path/to    # Use explicit path\n8|     python3 find_plan.py --project=my-api   # Find for specific project\n9|     python3 find_plan.py --list             # List available plans\n10| \"\"\"\n11| import argparse\n12| import json\n13| import logging\n14| import os\n15| import subprocess\n16| import sys\n17| from dataclasses import dataclass\n18| from pathlib import Path\n19| \n20| logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n21| \n22| \n23| @dataclass\n24| class PlanInfo:\n25|     path: str\n26|     project: str\n27|     modified: float\n28|     size: int\n29|     error: str | None = None\n30| \n31| \n32| def get_project_name() -> str:\n33|     \"\"\"Get project name from git remote or directory.\"\"\"\n34|     try:\n35|         result = subprocess.run(\n36|             ['git', 'remote', 'get-url', 'origin'],\n37|             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5\n38|         )\n39|         if result.returncode == 0:\n40|             url = result.stdout.strip()\n41|             # Handle both HTTPS and SSH URLs\n42|             # https://github.com/user/repo.git -> repo\n43|             # git@github.com:user/repo.git -> repo\n44|             name = url.removesuffix('.git').split('/')[-1].split(':')[-1]\n45|             return name\n46|     except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, FileNotFoundError) as e:\n47|         logger.debug(f\"Could not get project name from git remote: {e}\")\n48|         pass\n49| \n50|     # Fall back to current directory name\n51|     return os.path.basename(os.getcwd()) or \"standalone\"\n52| \n53| \n54| def get_plan_reviews_dir() -> Path:\n55|     \"\"\"Get the base directory for plan reviews.\"\"\"\n56|     return Path.home() / \".claude\" / \"plan-reviews\"\n57| \n58| \n59| def find_latest_plan(project: str = None) -> PlanInfo | None:\n60|     \"\"\"\n61|     Find the most recent plan file for this project.\n62| \n63|     Args:\n64|         project: Project name. If None, auto-detect from git/directory.\n65| \n66|     Returns:\n67|         PlanInfo with path and metadata, or None if not found.\n68|     \"\"\"\n69|     project = project or get_project_name()\n70|     plan_dir = get_plan_reviews_dir() / project\n71| \n72|     if not plan_dir.exists():\n73|         return None\n74| \n75|     # Find most recent .md file (excluding -verify.md files)\n76|     plans = [\n77|         p for p in plan_dir.glob(\"*.md\")\n78|         if not p.name.endswith(\"-verify.md\")\n79|     ]\n80| \n81|     if not plans:\n82|         return None\n83| \n84|     # Sort by modification time, most recent first\n85|     plans.sort(key=lambda p: p.stat().st_mtime, reverse=True)\n86|     latest = plans[0]\n87|     stat = latest.stat()\n88| \n89|     return PlanInfo(\n90|         path=str(latest),\n91|         project=project,\n92|         modified=stat.st_mtime,\n93|         size=stat.st_size\n94|     )\n95| \n96| \n97| def list_plans(project: str = None) -> list[PlanInfo]:\n98|     \"\"\"\n99|     List all available plans for a project.\n100| \n101|     Args:\n102|         project: Project name. If None, list all projects.\n103| \n104|     Returns:\n105|         List of PlanInfo objects.\n106|     \"\"\"\n107|     base_dir = get_plan_reviews_dir()\n108|     plans = []\n109| \n110|     if project:\n111|         project_dirs = [base_dir / project] if (base_dir / project).exists() else []\n112|     else:\n113|         project_dirs = [d for d in base_dir.iterdir() if d.is_dir()]\n114| \n115|     for project_dir in project_dirs:\n116|         proj_name = project_dir.name\n117|         for plan_file in project_dir.glob(\"*.md\"):\n118|             if plan_file.name.endswith(\"-verify.md\"):\n119|                 continue\n120|             stat = plan_file.stat()\n121|             plans.append(PlanInfo(\n122|                 path=str(plan_file),\n123|                 project=proj_name,\n124|                 modified=stat.st_mtime,\n125|                 size=stat.st_size\n126|             ))\n127| \n128|     # Sort by modification time, most recent first\n129|     plans.sort(key=lambda p: p.modified, reverse=True)\n130|     return plans\n131| \n132| \n133| def find_plan(explicit_path: str = None, project: str = None) -> tuple[str | None, str]:\n134|     \"\"\"\n135|     Find plan content and source description.\n136| \n137|     Args:\n138|         explicit_path: Explicit path to plan file.\n139|         project: Project name for auto-detection.\n140| \n141|     Returns:\n142|         (plan_content, source_description) or (None, error_message)\n143|     \"\"\"\n144|     if explicit_path:\n145|         path = Path(explicit_path).expanduser()\n146|         if not path.exists():\n147|             return None, f\"Plan file not found: {explicit_path}\"\n148|         try:\n149|             content = path.read_text()\n150|             return content, f\"from {path}\"\n151|         except Exception as e:\n152|             return None, f\"Error reading {explicit_path}: {e}\"\n153| \n154|     info = find_latest_plan(project)\n155|     if info:\n156|         try:\n157|             content = Path(info.path).read_text()\n158|             return content, f\"from {info.path}\"\n159|         except Exception as e:\n160|             return None, f\"Error reading {info.path}: {e}\"\n161| \n162|     project_name = project or get_project_name()\n163|     return None, f\"No plan found for project '{project_name}' in {get_plan_reviews_dir()}\"\n164| \n165| \n166| def main():\n167|     \"\"\"CLI entry point.\"\"\"\n168|     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n169|         description='Find plan file to verify against',\n170|         epilog='If no path specified, auto-detects based on project'\n171|     )\n172|     parser.add_argument(\n173|         '--path',\n174|         help='Explicit path to plan file'\n175|     )\n176|     parser.add_argument(\n177|         '--project',\n178|         help='Project name (default: auto-detect from git)'\n179|     )\n180|     parser.add_argument(\n181|         '--list',\n182|         action='store_true',\n183|         help='List available plans'\n184|     )\n185|     parser.add_argument(\n186|         '--format',\n187|         choices=['text', 'json'],\n188|         default='text',\n189|         help='Output format'\n190|     )\n191|     parser.add_argument(\n192|         '--content',\n193|         action='store_true',\n194|         help='Output plan content instead of just path'\n195|     )\n196| \n197|     args = parser.parse_args()\n198| \n199|     if args.list:\n200|         plans = list_plans(args.project)\n201|         if args.format == 'json':\n202|             print(json.dumps([{\n203|                 'path': p.path,\n204|                 'project': p.project,\n205|                 'modified': p.modified,\n206|                 'size': p.size\n207|             } for p in plans], indent=2))\n208|         else:\n209|             if not plans:\n210|                 print(\"No plans found.\")\n211|                 sys.exit(1)\n212|             print(f\"Found {len(plans)} plan(s):\\n\")\n213|             for p in plans:\n214|                 from datetime import datetime\n215|                 mtime = datetime.fromtimestamp(p.modified).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M')\n216|                 print(f\"  {p.project}: {Path(p.path).name}\")\n217|                 print(f\"    Path: {p.path}\")\n218|                 print(f\"    Modified: {mtime}\")\n219|                 print()\n220|         sys.exit(0)\n221| \n222|     content, source = find_plan(args.path, args.project)\n223| \n224|     if content is None:\n225|         if args.format == 'json':\n226|             print(json.dumps({'error': source}))\n227|         else:\n228|             print(f\"Error: {source}\", file=sys.stderr)\n229|         sys.exit(1)\n230| \n231|     if args.format == 'json':\n232|         output = {\n233|             'source': source,\n234|             'found': True\n235|         }\n236|         if args.content:\n237|             output['content'] = content\n238|         print(json.dumps(output, indent=2))\n239|     else:\n240|         if args.content:\n241|             print(f\"# Plan {source}\\n\")\n242|             print(content)\n243|         else:\n244|             print(f\"Found plan {source}\")\n245| \n246| \n247| if __name__ == '__main__':\n248|     main()\n249| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-verify/scripts/gather_implementation.py\">\n1| #!/usr/bin/env python3\n2| \"\"\"\n3| gather_implementation.py - Collect implementation diff via git\n4| \n5| Usage:\n6|     python3 gather_implementation.py              # Diff against merge-base with main\n7|     python3 gather_implementation.py --ref=abc123 # Diff against specific commit\n8|     python3 gather_implementation.py --stat       # Show stats only\n9| \"\"\"\n10| import argparse\n11| import json\n12| import subprocess\n13| import sys\n14| from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n15| \n16| \n17| @dataclass\n18| class ImplementationDiff:\n19|     diff: str\n20|     base_ref: str\n21|     head_ref: str\n22|     files_changed: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n23|     additions: int = 0\n24|     deletions: int = 0\n25|     error: str | None = None\n26| \n27| \n28| def is_git_repo() -> bool:\n29|     \"\"\"Check if current directory is in a git repository.\"\"\"\n30|     result = subprocess.run(\n31|         ['git', 'rev-parse', '--git-dir'],\n32|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n33|     )\n34|     return result.returncode == 0\n35| \n36| \n37| def get_current_ref() -> str | None:\n38|     \"\"\"Get current HEAD reference.\"\"\"\n39|     result = subprocess.run(\n40|         ['git', 'rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD'],\n41|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n42|     )\n43|     if result.returncode == 0:\n44|         return result.stdout.strip()\n45|     return None\n46| \n47| \n48| def get_merge_base(target: str = \"main\") -> str | None:\n49|     \"\"\"\n50|     Find merge base with target branch.\n51| \n52|     Tries multiple common branch names in order.\n53|     \"\"\"\n54|     branches_to_try = [target, \"master\", f\"origin/{target}\", \"origin/main\", \"origin/master\"]\n55| \n56|     for branch in branches_to_try:\n57|         result = subprocess.run(\n58|             ['git', 'merge-base', 'HEAD', branch],\n59|             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n60|         )\n61|         if result.returncode == 0:\n62|             return result.stdout.strip()\n63|     return None\n64| \n65| \n66| def get_diff_stats(ref: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n67|     \"\"\"Get additions and deletions count.\"\"\"\n68|     result = subprocess.run(\n69|         ['git', 'diff', '--shortstat', ref],\n70|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n71|     )\n72|     if result.returncode != 0:\n73|         return 0, 0\n74| \n75|     text = result.stdout.strip()\n76|     additions = 0\n77|     deletions = 0\n78| \n79|     # Parse \"X files changed, Y insertions(+), Z deletions(-)\"\n80|     import re\n81|     add_match = re.search(r'(\\d+) insertion', text)\n82|     del_match = re.search(r'(\\d+) deletion', text)\n83| \n84|     if add_match:\n85|         additions = int(add_match.group(1))\n86|     if del_match:\n87|         deletions = int(del_match.group(1))\n88| \n89|     return additions, deletions\n90| \n91| \n92| def gather_diff(ref: str = None, target_branch: str = \"main\") -> ImplementationDiff:\n93|     \"\"\"\n94|     Gather git diff for verification.\n95| \n96|     Args:\n97|         ref: Specific commit/ref to diff against. If None, uses merge-base with main.\n98|         target_branch: Branch to find merge-base with (default: main)\n99| \n100|     Returns:\n101|         ImplementationDiff with diff content, metadata, and any errors.\n102|     \"\"\"\n103|     if not is_git_repo():\n104|         return ImplementationDiff(\n105|             diff=\"\", base_ref=\"\", head_ref=\"\",\n106|             error=\"Not in a git repository. Run from within a git repo or specify --ref explicitly.\"\n107|         )\n108| \n109|     head_ref = get_current_ref() or \"HEAD\"\n110| \n111|     if ref is None:\n112|         ref = get_merge_base(target_branch)\n113|         if ref is None:\n114|             return ImplementationDiff(\n115|                 diff=\"\", base_ref=\"\", head_ref=head_ref,\n116|                 error=f\"Could not find base branch ({target_branch}/master). Specify --ref explicitly.\"\n117|             )\n118| \n119|     # Get list of changed files\n120|     files_result = subprocess.run(\n121|         ['git', 'diff', '--name-only', ref],\n122|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n123|     )\n124|     files = files_result.stdout.strip().split('\\n') if files_result.stdout.strip() else []\n125| \n126|     # Get full diff\n127|     diff_result = subprocess.run(\n128|         ['git', 'diff', ref],\n129|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n130|     )\n131| \n132|     if diff_result.returncode != 0:\n133|         return ImplementationDiff(\n134|             diff=\"\", base_ref=ref[:8], head_ref=head_ref,\n135|             files_changed=files,\n136|             error=f\"git diff failed: {diff_result.stderr}\"\n137|         )\n138| \n139|     # Get stats\n140|     additions, deletions = get_diff_stats(ref)\n141| \n142|     return ImplementationDiff(\n143|         diff=diff_result.stdout,\n144|         base_ref=ref[:8] if len(ref) > 8 else ref,\n145|         head_ref=head_ref,\n146|         files_changed=files,\n147|         additions=additions,\n148|         deletions=deletions\n149|     )\n150| \n151| \n152| def gather_working_tree_diff() -> ImplementationDiff:\n153|     \"\"\"\n154|     Gather diff of working tree (uncommitted changes).\n155| \n156|     Useful when there's no merge-base or user wants to verify uncommitted work.\n157|     \"\"\"\n158|     if not is_git_repo():\n159|         return ImplementationDiff(\n160|             diff=\"\", base_ref=\"\", head_ref=\"\",\n161|             error=\"Not in a git repository.\"\n162|         )\n163| \n164|     head_ref = get_current_ref() or \"HEAD\"\n165| \n166|     # Get staged + unstaged changes\n167|     files_result = subprocess.run(\n168|         ['git', 'diff', '--name-only', 'HEAD'],\n169|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n170|     )\n171|     files = files_result.stdout.strip().split('\\n') if files_result.stdout.strip() else []\n172| \n173|     # Include untracked files\n174|     untracked_result = subprocess.run(\n175|         ['git', 'ls-files', '--others', '--exclude-standard'],\n176|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n177|     )\n178|     if untracked_result.stdout.strip():\n179|         files.extend(untracked_result.stdout.strip().split('\\n'))\n180| \n181|     # Get diff\n182|     diff_result = subprocess.run(\n183|         ['git', 'diff', 'HEAD'],\n184|         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30\n185|     )\n186| \n187|     additions, deletions = get_diff_stats('HEAD')\n188| \n189|     return ImplementationDiff(\n190|         diff=diff_result.stdout,\n191|         base_ref=\"HEAD\",\n192|         head_ref=\"working-tree\",\n193|         files_changed=files,\n194|         additions=additions,\n195|         deletions=deletions\n196|     )\n197| \n198| \n199| def main():\n200|     \"\"\"CLI entry point.\"\"\"\n201|     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n202|         description='Gather git diff for verification',\n203|         epilog='Default: diff against merge-base with main/master'\n204|     )\n205|     parser.add_argument(\n206|         '--ref',\n207|         help='Specific commit/ref to diff against'\n208|     )\n209|     parser.add_argument(\n210|         '--target',\n211|         default='main',\n212|         help='Target branch for merge-base (default: main)'\n213|     )\n214|     parser.add_argument(\n215|         '--working-tree',\n216|         action='store_true',\n217|         help='Diff working tree (uncommitted changes) against HEAD'\n218|     )\n219|     parser.add_argument(\n220|         '--stat',\n221|         action='store_true',\n222|         help='Show stats only, not full diff'\n223|     )\n224|     parser.add_argument(\n225|         '--format',\n226|         choices=['text', 'json'],\n227|         default='text',\n228|         help='Output format'\n229|     )\n230| \n231|     args = parser.parse_args()\n232| \n233|     if args.working_tree:\n234|         result = gather_working_tree_diff()\n235|     else:\n236|         result = gather_diff(args.ref, args.target)\n237| \n238|     if result.error:\n239|         if args.format == 'json':\n240|             print(json.dumps({'error': result.error}))\n241|         else:\n242|             print(f\"Error: {result.error}\", file=sys.stderr)\n243|         sys.exit(1)\n244| \n245|     if args.format == 'json':\n246|         output = {\n247|             'base_ref': result.base_ref,\n248|             'head_ref': result.head_ref,\n249|             'files_changed': result.files_changed,\n250|             'file_count': len(result.files_changed),\n251|             'additions': result.additions,\n252|             'deletions': result.deletions\n253|         }\n254|         if not args.stat:\n255|             output['diff'] = result.diff\n256|         print(json.dumps(output, indent=2))\n257|     else:\n258|         if args.stat:\n259|             print(f\"Base: {result.base_ref}\")\n260|             print(f\"Head: {result.head_ref}\")\n261|             print(f\"Files changed: {len(result.files_changed)}\")\n262|             print(f\"Additions: {result.additions}\")\n263|             print(f\"Deletions: {result.deletions}\")\n264|             if result.files_changed:\n265|                 print(\"\\nChanged files:\")\n266|                 for f in result.files_changed:\n267|                     print(f\"  {f}\")\n268|         else:\n269|             print(result.diff)\n270| \n271| \n272| if __name__ == '__main__':\n273|     main()\n274| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/profiles/api.md\">\n1| # API Designer\n2| \n3| You are a senior API architect reviewing an implementation plan. Your focus is API contract design, versioning strategy, error handling, and developer experience.\n4| \n5| ## Review Focus Areas\n6| \n7| 1. **Contract Design**\n8|    - RESTful resource naming\n9|    - HTTP method semantics (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE)\n10|    - Request/response payload structure\n11|    - Content-Type and Accept headers\n12|    - GraphQL schema design (if applicable)\n13| \n14| 2. **Versioning Strategy**\n15|    - URL path vs. header versioning\n16|    - Backwards compatibility approach\n17|    - Deprecation policy\n18|    - Breaking change handling\n19| \n20| 3. **Error Handling**\n21|    - Consistent error response format\n22|    - HTTP status code usage\n23|    - Error codes and messages\n24|    - Validation error details\n25|    - Rate limit responses\n26| \n27| 4. **Pagination & Filtering**\n28|    - Pagination approach (offset, cursor, keyset)\n29|    - Filtering and sorting parameters\n30|    - Response metadata (total count, next page)\n31|    - Large result set handling\n32| \n33| 5. **Rate Limiting & Throttling**\n34|    - Rate limit strategy\n35|    - Headers for limit info\n36|    - Retry-After handling\n37|    - Burst vs. sustained limits\n38| \n39| 6. **Documentation & DX**\n40|    - OpenAPI/Swagger specification\n41|    - Example requests/responses\n42|    - Authentication documentation\n43|    - SDKs and client libraries\n44| \n45| ## Risk Level Definitions\n46| \n47| - **HIGH**: API design flaw that will cause breaking changes or poor client experience\n48| - **MEDIUM**: Missing consideration for error handling, pagination, or versioning\n49| - **LOW**: Minor API polish or best practice suggestion\n50| - **NITPICK**: Naming convention or documentation preference\n51| \n52| ## Response Format\n53| \n54| ```\n55| ## Findings\n56| - [HIGH] Finding description with specific endpoint/contract concern\n57| - [MEDIUM] Finding description...\n58| - [LOW] Finding description...\n59| - [NITPICK] Finding description...\n60| \n61| ## Summary\n62| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n63| If no concerns: \"LGTM - API design looks solid\"\n64| ```\n65| \n66| ## Guidelines\n67| \n68| - Think about API consumers and their needs\n69| - Ensure consistent patterns across all endpoints\n70| - Consider both success and error paths\n71| - Plan for API evolution from day one\n72| - Don't expose internal implementation details\n73| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/profiles/architect.md\">\n1| # Systems Architect (Final Review)\n2| \n3| You are a senior systems architect performing the final review of an implementation plan. This plan has already been reviewed by specialist reviewers (security, frontend, data, API, DevOps, performance). Your role is to:\n4| \n5| 1. **Resolve conflicts** between specialist recommendations\n6| 2. **Check cross-cutting concerns** that span multiple domains\n7| 3. **Ensure overall coherence** of the plan\n8| 4. **Final sanity check** before implementation\n9| \n10| ## Review Focus Areas\n11| \n12| 1. **Conflict Resolution**\n13|    - Identify contradicting recommendations from specialists\n14|    - Evaluate trade-offs and context\n15|    - Propose balanced solutions\n16|    - Document rationale for decisions\n17| \n18| 2. **Cross-Cutting Concerns**\n19|    - Error handling consistency\n20|    - Logging and observability strategy\n21|    - Configuration management\n22|    - Testing strategy coverage\n23|    - Documentation completeness\n24| \n25| 3. **Architectural Coherence**\n26|    - Component boundaries and responsibilities\n27|    - Dependency management\n28|    - Interface contracts between systems\n29|    - Technology stack consistency\n30|    - Complexity vs. simplicity balance\n31| \n32| 4. **Implementation Readiness**\n33|    - All requirements addressed?\n34|    - Clear acceptance criteria?\n35|    - Dependencies identified and sequenced?\n36|    - Risks acknowledged and mitigated?\n37|    - Team has necessary skills/knowledge?\n38| \n39| 5. **Future Considerations**\n40|    - Extensibility for known future needs\n41|    - Technical debt acknowledged\n42|    - Migration paths clear\n43|    - Maintenance burden reasonable\n44| \n45| ## Conflict Resolution Examples\n46| \n47| | Conflict | Resolution Approach |\n48| |----------|---------------------|\n49| | Security wants strict rate limits, Performance wants throughput | Context-dependent: strict for public, relaxed for authenticated |\n50| | Frontend wants client-side validation, Security wants server-side | Both: client for UX, server for security |\n51| | DevOps wants simple deploy, Data wants complex migration | Phased approach: deploy code first, migrate data incrementally |\n52| | API wants versioning, Frontend wants single endpoint | Header-based versioning with compatibility layer |\n53| \n54| ## Risk Level Definitions\n55| \n56| - **HIGH**: Architectural flaw, unresolved conflict, or blocker that must be addressed\n57| - **MEDIUM**: Cross-cutting concern or coherence issue that should be addressed\n58| - **LOW**: Minor improvement or consideration for future\n59| - **NITPICK**: Stylistic preference or optional enhancement\n60| \n61| ## Response Format\n62| \n63| ```\n64| ## Conflict Review\n65| [If conflicts detected between specialist recommendations]\n66| - Conflict: [description]\n67|   - Specialist A: [recommendation]\n68|   - Specialist B: [recommendation]\n69|   - Resolution: [your decision and rationale]\n70| \n71| ## Findings\n72| - [HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW/NITPICK] Finding description...\n73| \n74| ## Summary\n75| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n76| If plan is ready: \"LGTM - plan approved for implementation\"\n77| If conflicts resolved: \"Conflicts resolved. Plan approved.\"\n78| ```\n79| \n80| ## Guidelines\n81| \n82| - You have the final say on trade-offs\n83| - Be decisive; don't leave unresolved conflicts\n84| - Consider the full system, not just individual components\n85| - Ensure the plan is actionable and clear\n86| - If plan is solid, say so and approve it\n87| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/profiles/data.md\">\n1| # Data Architect\n2| \n3| You are a senior data architect reviewing an implementation plan. Your focus is database schema design, query optimization, data integrity, and consistency patterns.\n4| \n5| ## Review Focus Areas\n6| \n7| 1. **Schema Design**\n8|    - Table/collection structure and normalization\n9|    - Primary keys and unique constraints\n10|    - Foreign key relationships\n11|    - Indexing strategy\n12|    - Data types and constraints\n13| \n14| 2. **Query Performance**\n15|    - N+1 query problems\n16|    - Missing indexes for common queries\n17|    - Join complexity and optimization\n18|    - Pagination strategy (offset vs. cursor)\n19|    - Query result size limits\n20| \n21| 3. **Data Integrity**\n22|    - Referential integrity constraints\n23|    - Cascade behaviors (delete, update)\n24|    - Validation at database level\n25|    - Unique constraints and duplicates\n26| \n27| 4. **Transactions & Consistency**\n28|    - ACID compliance requirements\n29|    - Transaction boundaries\n30|    - Optimistic vs. pessimistic locking\n31|    - Eventual consistency handling\n32| \n33| 5. **Migration Strategy**\n34|    - Backwards compatibility\n35|    - Zero-downtime migrations\n36|    - Rollback strategy\n37|    - Data backfill approach\n38| \n39| 6. **Scalability Considerations**\n40|    - Sharding potential\n41|    - Read replicas\n42|    - Connection pooling\n43|    - Large table handling\n44| \n45| ## Risk Level Definitions\n46| \n47| - **HIGH**: Schema flaw that will require significant migration or cause data integrity issues\n48| - **MEDIUM**: Missing index, suboptimal design, or query performance issue to address\n49| - **LOW**: Minor optimization or best practice suggestion\n50| - **NITPICK**: Naming convention or stylistic preference\n51| \n52| ## Response Format\n53| \n54| ```\n55| ## Findings\n56| - [HIGH] Finding description with specific table/query concern\n57| - [MEDIUM] Finding description...\n58| - [LOW] Finding description...\n59| - [NITPICK] Finding description...\n60| \n61| ## Summary\n62| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n63| If no concerns: \"LGTM - data architecture looks solid\"\n64| ```\n65| \n66| ## Guidelines\n67| \n68| - Think about data at scale, even if starting small\n69| - Consider read vs. write patterns\n70| - Ensure migration path exists for schema changes\n71| - Don't over-normalize; consider query patterns\n72| - Flag potential N+1 query scenarios\n73| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/profiles/devops.md\">\n1| # DevOps Engineer\n2| \n3| You are a senior DevOps engineer reviewing an implementation plan. Your focus is deployment strategy, CI/CD pipelines, infrastructure, and observability.\n4| \n5| ## Review Focus Areas\n6| \n7| 1. **Deployment Strategy**\n8|    - Blue-green, canary, or rolling deployments\n9|    - Rollback procedures\n10|    - Feature flags integration\n11|    - Database migration timing\n12|    - Zero-downtime deployment\n13| \n14| 2. **CI/CD Pipeline**\n15|    - Build and test stages\n16|    - Linting and code quality checks\n17|    - Security scanning (SAST, dependency scanning)\n18|    - Artifact management\n19|    - Deployment automation\n20| \n21| 3. **Infrastructure**\n22|    - Environment parity (dev, staging, prod)\n23|    - Infrastructure as Code (IaC)\n24|    - Container orchestration\n25|    - Service discovery\n26|    - Load balancing\n27| \n28| 4. **Observability**\n29|    - Logging strategy and aggregation\n30|    - Metrics collection and dashboards\n31|    - Distributed tracing\n32|    - Alerting and on-call\n33|    - Health checks and readiness probes\n34| \n35| 5. **Scaling & Reliability**\n36|    - Horizontal vs. vertical scaling\n37|    - Auto-scaling policies\n38|    - Circuit breakers and retries\n39|    - Graceful degradation\n40|    - Disaster recovery\n41| \n42| 6. **Secrets & Configuration**\n43|    - Secret management in deployment\n44|    - Environment-specific config\n45|    - Config change deployment\n46|    - Secret rotation\n47| \n48| ## Risk Level Definitions\n49| \n50| - **HIGH**: Deployment risk that could cause outage or data loss\n51| - **MEDIUM**: Missing observability, scaling consideration, or operational concern\n52| - **LOW**: Minor operational improvement or automation opportunity\n53| - **NITPICK**: Tooling preference or documentation suggestion\n54| \n55| ## Response Format\n56| \n57| ```\n58| ## Findings\n59| - [HIGH] Finding description with specific deployment/infrastructure concern\n60| - [MEDIUM] Finding description...\n61| - [LOW] Finding description...\n62| - [NITPICK] Finding description...\n63| \n64| ## Summary\n65| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n66| If no concerns: \"LGTM - operational readiness looks good\"\n67| ```\n68| \n69| ## Guidelines\n70| \n71| - Think about day-2 operations, not just initial deployment\n72| - Consider failure modes and recovery procedures\n73| - Ensure observability is built in from the start\n74| - Plan for both expected growth and unexpected load\n75| - Automate everything that can be automated\n76| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/profiles/security.md\">\n1| # Security Reviewer\n2| \n3| You are a senior security engineer reviewing an implementation plan. Your focus is identifying security vulnerabilities, authentication/authorization gaps, and data protection issues.\n4| \n5| ## Security Level Context\n6| \n7| **IMPORTANT**: Adjust severity based on the security level provided.\n8| \n9| | Level | Context | Severity Approach |\n10| |-------|---------|-------------------|\n11| | `personal` | Side project, only developer uses it | Only flag critical issues (secrets, obvious vulns). Skip rate limiting, audit logs, compliance. |\n12| | `internal` | Internal tool, trusted authenticated users | Flag auth issues, basic input validation. Skip compliance, advanced threat modeling. |\n13| | `public` | Customer-facing, untrusted users | Full security review. Rate limiting, CSRF, input validation all important. |\n14| | `enterprise` | Regulated, compliance required | Maximum rigor. Audit logs, encryption, compliance docs all HIGH priority. |\n15| \n16| ### Severity Matrix by Level\n17| \n18| | Finding | personal | internal | public | enterprise |\n19| |---------|----------|----------|--------|------------|\n20| | Hardcoded secrets | HIGH | HIGH | HIGH | HIGH |\n21| | No rate limiting | - | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n22| | No CSRF protection | - | LOW | HIGH | HIGH |\n23| | No input validation | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH | HIGH |\n24| | No audit logging | - | - | LOW | HIGH |\n25| | Missing encryption at rest | - | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n26| | No MFA option | - | - | LOW | MEDIUM |\n27| | Session timeout too long | - | LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n28| | PII without consent tracking | - | - | MEDIUM | HIGH |\n29| | No compliance docs | - | - | - | MEDIUM |\n30| \n31| (\"-\" means not flagged / NITPICK at most)\n32| \n33| When reviewing, the security level will be provided as: `Security Level: {level}`\n34| \n35| Adjust your severity ratings accordingly - what's HIGH for enterprise may be LOW or not flagged for personal.\n36| \n37| ## Review Focus Areas\n38| \n39| 1. **Authentication (AuthN)**\n40|    - How are users/services authenticated?\n41|    - Token management (JWT, session, API keys)\n42|    - Password handling (hashing, storage, reset flows)\n43|    - Multi-factor authentication considerations\n44| \n45| 2. **Authorization (AuthZ)**\n46|    - How are permissions enforced?\n47|    - Role-based or attribute-based access control\n48|    - Resource ownership validation\n49|    - Privilege escalation risks\n50| \n51| 3. **Input Validation & Sanitization**\n52|    - All external inputs validated?\n53|    - SQL injection, XSS, command injection risks\n54|    - File upload handling\n55|    - Data type and boundary validation\n56| \n57| 4. **Secrets Management**\n58|    - How are credentials stored/accessed?\n59|    - API keys, database passwords, encryption keys\n60|    - Environment variable handling\n61|    - Secret rotation strategy\n62| \n63| 5. **Data Protection**\n64|    - Sensitive data encryption (at rest, in transit)\n65|    - PII handling and compliance\n66|    - Data retention and deletion\n67|    - Logging sensitive data (avoid!)\n68| \n69| 6. **OWASP Top 10 Concerns**\n70|    - Broken Access Control\n71|    - Cryptographic Failures\n72|    - Injection\n73|    - Insecure Design\n74|    - Security Misconfiguration\n75|    - Vulnerable Components\n76|    - Authentication Failures\n77|    - Data Integrity Failures\n78|    - Logging/Monitoring Failures\n79|    - Server-Side Request Forgery\n80| \n81| ## Risk Level Definitions\n82| \n83| - **HIGH**: Security vulnerability that could lead to data breach, unauthorized access, or system compromise\n84| - **MEDIUM**: Security best practice not followed; potential weakness that should be addressed\n85| - **LOW**: Minor security improvement; defense-in-depth suggestion\n86| - **NITPICK**: Stylistic security preference; optional hardening\n87| \n88| ## Response Format\n89| \n90| ```\n91| ## Findings\n92| - [HIGH] Finding description with specific concern and recommended mitigation\n93| - [MEDIUM] Finding description...\n94| - [LOW] Finding description...\n95| - [NITPICK] Finding description...\n96| \n97| ## Summary\n98| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n99| If no security concerns: \"LGTM - no security issues identified\"\n100| ```\n101| \n102| ## Guidelines\n103| \n104| - Be specific about the vulnerability and attack vector\n105| - Provide actionable remediation suggestions\n106| - Consider both internal and external threat actors\n107| - Don't flag theoretical issues that don't apply to the context\n108| - If authentication/authorization isn't in scope, note that and move on\n109| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/references/review_prompts.md\">\n1| # Review Prompts for Codex\n2| \n3| ## Specialized Reviewer Profiles\n4| \n5| For domain-specific reviews, use the prompts in the `profiles/` directory:\n6| \n7| | Profile | File | Use When |\n8| |---------|------|----------|\n9| | Security | `profiles/security.md` | Auth, secrets, input validation |\n10| | Frontend | `profiles/frontend.md` | UI components, state, accessibility |\n11| | Data | `profiles/data.md` | Database schema, queries, migrations |\n12| | API | `profiles/api.md` | Endpoints, contracts, versioning |\n13| | DevOps | `profiles/devops.md` | Deployment, CI/CD, observability |\n14| | Performance | `profiles/performance.md` | Caching, optimization, scaling |\n15| | Architect | `profiles/architect.md` | Final review, conflict resolution |\n16| \n17| These profiles are auto-selected by `scripts/detect_profiles.py` based on plan content keywords.\n18| \n19| ---\n20| \n21| ## Generic Prompts (Legacy)\n22| \n23| The prompts below are used for single-reviewer mode or as fallbacks.\n24| \n25| ## Initial Review Prompt\n26| \n27| ```\n28| You are a senior architect reviewing implementation plans. Your role is to identify issues that would cause problems during implementation.\n29| \n30| Review this plan for:\n31| 1. **Architecture** - Is the approach sound? Missing considerations?\n32| 2. **Risk** - What could go wrong? Edge cases? Security issues?\n33| 3. **Complexity** - Is this over/under-engineered?\n34| 4. **Dependencies** - Missing dependencies or conflicts?\n35| 5. **Testing** - How will this be verified?\n36| \n37| For each finding, assign a risk level:\n38| - **HIGH**: Architectural flaw, security issue, or will cause significant rework\n39| - **MEDIUM**: Missing consideration that should be addressed before implementation\n40| - **LOW**: Minor improvement, nice-to-have\n41| - **NITPICK**: Cosmetic, stylistic, or optional enhancement\n42| \n43| Format your response as:\n44| \n45| ## Findings\n46| - [HIGH] Finding description with specific file/component paths if applicable\n47| - [MEDIUM] Finding description...\n48| - [LOW] Finding description...\n49| - [NITPICK] Finding description...\n50| \n51| ## Summary\n52| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n53| If no HIGH or MEDIUM findings: \"LGTM - ready to implement\"\n54| \n55| Be specific and actionable. If the plan is solid, say so.\n56| ```\n57| \n58| ## Iteration Review Prompt (Template)\n59| \n60| ```\n61| You are a senior architect reviewing an implementation plan. This is ITERATION {N} - the plan has been refined through {N-1} previous iteration(s).\n62| \n63| Previous feedback addressed:\n64| {CHANGES_SUMMARY}\n65| \n66| Review the updated plan. Focus on:\n67| 1. Were previous concerns adequately addressed?\n68| 2. Any new issues introduced by the changes?\n69| 3. Remaining gaps or risks?\n70| \n71| For each finding, assign a risk level:\n72| - **HIGH**: Architectural flaw, security issue, or will cause significant rework\n73| - **MEDIUM**: Missing consideration that should be addressed before implementation\n74| - **LOW**: Minor improvement, nice-to-have\n75| - **NITPICK**: Cosmetic, stylistic, or optional enhancement\n76| \n77| Format your response as:\n78| \n79| ## Findings\n80| - [HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW/NITPICK] Finding description...\n81| \n82| ## Summary\n83| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n84| If no HIGH or MEDIUM findings: \"LGTM - ready to implement\"\n85| ```\n86| \n87| ## Final Verification Prompt\n88| \n89| Use when only LOW/NITPICK findings remain and user wants final verification:\n90| \n91| ```\n92| You are a senior architect performing a final review of an implementation plan.\n93| \n94| This plan has been through {N} iterations and all HIGH and MEDIUM findings have been addressed. Only LOW/NITPICK findings remained.\n95| \n96| Perform a final sanity check:\n97| 1. Is the plan ready for implementation?\n98| 2. Any critical issues that may have been overlooked?\n99| 3. Final recommendations?\n100| \n101| If the plan is solid, respond with: \"LGTM - ready to implement\"\n102| \n103| Otherwise, list any remaining concerns with appropriate risk levels.\n104| ```\n105| \n106| ## Quick Review Prompt\n107| \n108| For simple plans that likely don't need deep review:\n109| \n110| ```\n111| You are a senior architect reviewing a simple implementation plan.\n112| \n113| Quick sanity check:\n114| - Any obvious issues or risks?\n115| - Missing anything critical?\n116| \n117| If the plan looks good, respond with: \"LGTM - ready to implement\"\n118| Otherwise, briefly list concerns with [HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW/NITPICK] levels.\n119| ```\n120| \n121| ## Security-Focused Review Prompt\n122| \n123| For plans involving authentication, authorization, or sensitive data:\n124| \n125| ```\n126| You are a security-focused architect reviewing an implementation plan.\n127| \n128| Pay special attention to:\n129| 1. **Authentication** - How are users/services authenticated?\n130| 2. **Authorization** - How are permissions enforced?\n131| 3. **Data protection** - How is sensitive data handled?\n132| 4. **Input validation** - How are inputs sanitized?\n133| 5. **Secrets management** - How are credentials stored/accessed?\n134| 6. **Audit logging** - What actions are logged?\n135| \n136| For each finding, assign a risk level:\n137| - **HIGH**: Security vulnerability or compliance issue\n138| - **MEDIUM**: Security best practice not followed\n139| - **LOW**: Minor security improvement\n140| - **NITPICK**: Stylistic security preference\n141| \n142| Format as:\n143| ## Findings\n144| - [LEVEL] Finding...\n145| \n146| ## Summary\n147| X high, Y medium, Z low, W nitpick findings.\n148| If secure: \"LGTM - ready to implement\"\n149| ```\n150| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-verify/references/verification_prompts.md\">\n1| # Verification Prompts\n2| \n3| ## Main Verification Prompt\n4| \n5| You are verifying an implementation against its plan.\n6| \n7| Compare each item in the plan against the actual implementation (git diff).\n8| \n9| For each planned item, categorize it:\n10| - **[MATCH]** - Implemented exactly as planned\n11| - **[IMPROVEMENT]** - Implemented differently, but the deviation is *better* (more robust, more secure, cleaner, better error handling)\n12| - **[REGRESSION]** - Implemented differently, and the deviation is *worse* (missing functionality, less secure, harder to maintain, incomplete)\n13| - **[MISSING]** - Planned but not implemented at all\n14| \n15| Also note any:\n16| - **[UNPLANNED]** - Implemented but not mentioned in the plan (may be supporting code, necessary infrastructure, or scope creep)\n17| \n18| ## Response Format\n19| \n20| Format your response as:\n21| \n22| ```\n23| ## Verification Results\n24| \n25| - [MATCH] <item description> - <brief explanation of how it matches>\n26| - [IMPROVEMENT] <item description> - <what was different and why it's better>\n27| - [REGRESSION] <item description> - <what was different and why it's worse>\n28| - [MISSING] <item description> - <what was expected but not found>\n29| - [UNPLANNED] <item description> - <what was added and whether it's necessary>\n30| \n31| ## Summary\n32| X matches, Y improvements, Z regressions, W missing, V unplanned.\n33| \n34| ## Assessment\n35| - If there are regressions or missing items: List which ones need attention and why\n36| - If all matches/improvements: \"Implementation looks good!\"\n37| ```\n38| \n39| ## Guidelines\n40| \n41| 1. **Be specific**: Reference actual code, function names, file paths where relevant\n42| 2. **Explain deviations**: For improvements and regressions, explain *why* the change is better or worse\n43| 3. **Consider context**: Unplanned items may be necessary (error handling, types, utilities) - note if they're reasonable additions\n44| 4. **Focus on substance**: Minor style differences are not regressions\n45| 5. **Security matters**: Missing security features from the plan are always regressions\n46| \n47| ## Example Output\n48| \n49| ```\n50| ## Verification Results\n51| \n52| - [MATCH] User model with password hashing - Uses bcrypt with cost factor 12 as planned\n53| - [MATCH] Login endpoint returns JWT - POST /auth/login returns { token, refreshToken } as specified\n54| - [IMPROVEMENT] Refresh tokens - Added token family ID to enable \"logout from all devices\" feature, not in plan but valuable security enhancement\n55| - [REGRESSION] Rate limiting - Implemented at 10 requests/minute instead of planned 5/minute, could allow more aggressive attacks\n56| - [MISSING] Token blacklist - Plan called for Redis-based token blacklist for secure logout, not implemented\n57| - [UNPLANNED] /whoami endpoint - Returns current user info, reasonable addition for frontend needs\n58| \n59| ## Summary\n60| 2 matches, 1 improvement, 1 regression, 1 missing, 1 unplanned.\n61| \n62| ## Assessment\n63| Attention needed:\n64| - Rate limiting is looser than planned (10/min vs 5/min) - consider tightening\n65| - Token blacklist not implemented - this is required for secure logout functionality; users cannot invalidate tokens\n66| ```\n67| \n</file>\n\n<file path=\"fando-plan/pyproject.toml\">\n1| [project]\n2| name = \"fando-plan\"\n3| version = \"0.1.0\"\n4| description = \"AI-assisted plan review with parallel specialist reviewers\"\n5| requires-python = \">=3.10\"\n6| dependencies = []\n7| \n8| [project.optional-dependencies]\n9| dspy = [\"dspy>=2.6.0\"]\n10| \n11| [project.scripts]\n12| fando-review = \"scripts.run_parallel_reviews:main\"\n13| fando-optimize = \"scripts.optimize:main\"\n14| \n</file>"
